Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: a keyed mutex for shared textures (cranvr)

SteamVR passes frames between its processes in textures made with SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX and orders the access with their
keyed mutexes. Chromium's GPU process asks the textures that it shares for the mutex too. DXMT gave no such interface.
A texture made with SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX now answers the query.

The GPU keeps the order, as on Windows. The processes of one texture share a Metal shared event and a count of the
releases in a small section of shared memory, both named after the texture's shared name. ReleaseSync has the GPU set
the event to the next count after the caller's commands. AcquireSync has the GPU wait for the last count before the
caller's next commands. Neither call waits for the GPU. A reader's process sets the event itself when a writer's GPU
has not set it within a second, so a writer that ends early does not stop the reader's GPU for good.

A first version waited in ReleaseSync until the GPU had finished. The Lab then waited 8.5 ms in each frame and
SteamVR's compositor 8.7 ms, and SteamVR made about 50 frames a second at 72 Hz. With the event it made 65 in a run
right after.
